// An unrecognised token in a class body must be a diagnostic, not a hang.
//
// The class-body loop in parse_class_decl_with_doc tests only `}` and `eof` in
// its condition, and each of its member branches advances only when it matches.
// Before the unconditional `else`, a token matching none of them left the parser
// position unchanged and the compiler spun forever — rc=124 under `timeout`,
// with no output to grep for.
//
// `let` is used deliberately: it is a real keyword, it is NOT valid as a class
// member (fields are declared with `var`), and it is not part of the visibility
// syntax. So this test keeps meaning exactly the same thing after
// `private`/`public` are recognised in this loop.
class Broken {
    let x: Int = 1
    fun init() {}
}

fun main(): Int {
    var b: Broken = Broken()
    return 0
}
